    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Background: The growing population of individuals with congenital heart disease (CHD) face an increased burden of early cardiovascular events, including a 13-fold risk of developing heart failure. Heart failure risk is greatest in those with the most severe forms of CHD such as those with single ventricle CHD palliated to a Fontan circulation. We assessed whether premature biologic aging is present in single ventricle heart disease with a Fontan circulation, risk factors associated with biologic aging and whether it predisposes to heart failure.\r\nMethods:  We collected demographic and clinical data, and blood from patients with a Fontan circulation and controls with structurally normal hearts following consent. We assessed the biologic age using telomere length (TL), a biomarker of aging. Telomeres are chromatin structures found at the ends of chromosomes that shorten with age. TL was measured using quantitative PCR from buffy coat DNA and expressed as a ratio of telomeric product/single copy gene product (T/S). Between-group differences were assessed using t-test, data presented as mean ± SD and P<0.05 was considered significant.\r\nResults: Mean age was 15.6±8.1 years in the Fontan cohort (N=122) vs. 18±15.8 years in controls (N=30). 63% were males in the Fontan cohort vs. 73% in controls. The Fontan cohort comprised of patients with atrioventricular septal defect (AVSD) (N=16), double outlet right ventricle (DORV, N=13), hypoplastic left heart syndrome (HLHS, N=49), double inlet left ventricle (DILV, N=21), pulmonary atresia (PA, N=6), tricuspid atresia (TA, N=17).  In both groups, there was a trend towards shorter TL with increasing age. The Fontan cohort had a trend toward shorter TL vs. controls (1.07±0.15 vs. 1.17±0.2; p=NS). The TL difference of 0.05 suggests that the Fontan cohort is 4.5 years older than controls. Patients with morphologic right ventricles had shorter TL vs. those with morphologic left ventricles (1.04±0.17 vs 1.10±0.2; p=0.03). TL by diagnosis was as follows from shortest to longest: HLHS 1.01±0.14; AVSD 1.08±0.12; DORV 1.08±0.15; DILV 1.10±0.11;  TA 1.10±0.16; and PA 1.27±0.14. The HLHS cohort had the shortest TL and the most co-morbidities: PLE (20.4%), pulmonary hypertension (6%), endocarditis (2%), CVA (12%) and cirrhosis (2%) compared to the other groups. Interestingly, there was no association between systemic ventricular function and TL. Interestingly, there was no association between echocardiogram qualitative ventricular function and TL.\r\nConclusions: We show for the first time, premature biologic aging in the Fontan circulation, its association with increased comorbidities and with single right ventricles being most at risk. Premature aging may predispose to heightened all-cause mortality and also predispose to early onset of age-related diseases.",
